  "scriptId": "src-04-perfect-squares-nearest-square",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Perfect Squares and Nearest-Square Problems",
    "description": "Recognise square structure and make the smallest possible change that turns a number into a perfect square.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build perfect-square judgement from prime-pair structure, then add the chapter’s digit clues, interval counting, digit-boundary squares, multiplier and divisor repairs, LCM-to-square construction, nearest-square gaps and a decimal gap capstone. Every “least” answer is justified by showing why no smaller change can reach a square.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach perfect-square recognition from even prime exponents and the chapter’s unit-digit clues, including that a square cannot end in 8 and that squares ending in 1 come from unit digits 1 or 9. Cover counting perfect squares in an interval, the greatest and least n-digit perfect squares, least multipliers and divisors that repair odd prime exponents, the least perfect square divisible by several given numbers by starting from their LCM, and least additions or subtractions to reach the nearest square. Include the same reasoning for a small decimal perfect-square adjustment. Make minimality explicit: identify the neighbouring square first, then take the gap.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 33"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn to recognise a perfect square before we calculate its root.\n\nA square has structure: prime factors pair up, nearby squares create clear gaps, and even the last digit can sometimes rule an answer out immediately.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A perfect square has no lonely prime factor.\n\nExample:\n3600 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5²\n\nAll exponents are even, so every prime can be paired.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "That gives a fast test:\n\nall prime exponents even → perfect square\nany odd exponent → not a perfect square\n\nThe rule comes directly from the factor-pair method.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
